    "seo": {
      "title": "Seasonal Foods in Iran: Fresh Ingredients and Preserved Harvests",
      "description": "Explore regional seasonal cooking in Iran, from Gilan's spring dishes to dried fruit, preserved harvests, and changing kitchen technologies."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "regional-seasons",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Many environments, many food calendars",
        "content": "Seasonal food in Iran is best understood through regional examples rather than a fixed nationwide timetable. Differences in latitude, elevation, and climate support fruits ranging from dates in warm regions to orchard fruits associated with temperate and colder environments. That diversity makes the place of production essential context: a list of Iranian ingredients alone cannot establish when each becomes locally available. [s1]\n\nSeasonality also includes what people keep after a harvest. The Persian term *khoshkbar* covers nuts and dried produce, bringing together foods with different methods of storage and preparation. A winter table can therefore contain the products of earlier seasons without depending entirely on freshly harvested ingredients. Freshness and seasonality are related, but they are not interchangeable. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "gilan-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Spring and winter in Gilan",
        "content": "Gilan, on Iran's Caspian coast, provides a particularly detailed documented example. Ethnographer Christian Bromberger describes a culinary transition from richer winter preparations toward greener spring dishes. *Baqala qatoq*, a preparation of beans, herbs, and eggs, belongs to this spring repertoire; raw herbs accompany meals during spring and summer. Cold-served eggplant preparations also feature in the warmer part of the year. [s2]\n\nThe account includes the categories *garm* and *sard*, conventionally translated as “hot” and “cold.” These describe a traditional dietary classification rather than simply the temperature at which a dish is served. They help explain local menu choices, but should not be treated as established medical guidance. The same study records differences between the Caspian plain and Gilan's drier areas, warning against treating a regional cuisine as internally uniform. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "preserved-harvest",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Extending the harvest",
        "content": "Drying gives fruit another culinary life. Documented products include raisins, dried figs, sour cherries, and apricots. *Barga* refers to dried fruit halves, notably peach or apricot halves that have been stoned before drying. Such foods demonstrate that preservation is part of seasonal cuisine itself, rather than an exception to it. [s1]\n\nSour cherries offer a focused example: Encyclopaedia Iranica records dried sour cherries as an ingredient of sweet nut-and-fruit mixtures and as a winter snack, particularly for children. This is a documented food custom, not evidence that all households follow the same practice today. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "changing-technologies",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How technology changes the calendar",
        "content": "Freezing changes the relationship between harvest time and cooking time. Bromberger's account explicitly identifies freezing raw and cooked ingredients as a change to the seasonal rhythm and organization of cooking in Gilan. Its observations should be read within the period documented, rather than as a survey of present-day kitchens. [s2]\n\nHistorical cooling technology also needs careful interpretation. A *yakhchal*, or traditional ice-house, stored winter ice for summer distribution. Research on Iranian ice-houses distinguishes that function from refrigerating food inside the building. Calling every yakhchal an ancient food refrigerator obscures how the system actually worked. [s4]\n\nTogether, these examples suggest a useful way to read an Iranian seasonal meal: ask where its ingredients came from, whether they were fresh or preserved, and which regional customs shaped their preparation. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
