    "description": "Iranian foods associated with New Year gatherings, weddings, and other social occasions connect hospitality with ceremonial meaning. The same ingredients and dishes can also appear in religious settings, so their significance depends on the occasion and the people preparing them.",
    "tags": ["Ceremonial food", "Nowruz", "Haft-sin", "Weddings", "Hospitality", "Ajil"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:26:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:26:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Secular Ceremonial Foods in Iran: Nowruz, Weddings and Hospitality",
      "description": "Discover foods associated with Iranian New Year gatherings and weddings, and learn why ceremonial meaning depends on context."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope-and-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Food acquires meaning through an occasion",
        "content": "“Secular ceremonial foods” is a useful editorial grouping for foods shared at social and calendar occasions, but it is not a rigid indigenous classification. Historical accounts distinguish ordinary hospitality from explicitly devotional meals, while also showing that ingredients and dishes move between these settings. A food's appearance at a wedding or New Year gathering does not make it exclusively secular. [s1][s2]\n\nThe distinction matters particularly for *ajil*, an assortment of nuts, seeds, and sometimes dried fruit. Encyclopaedia Iranica records it at receptions, informal gatherings, picnics, New Year celebrations, and votive offerings. Its social role cannot be inferred from its ingredients alone. The relevant questions are who offers it, to whom, and on what occasion. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "nowruz-display",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The New Year display and the meal",
        "content": "*Nowruz*, the Iranian New Year festival, brings together foods for display, hospitality, and eating. The *haft-sin* is a ceremonial arrangement conventionally organized around items whose Persian names begin with the letter sin. Apples, garlic, vinegar, and *samanu*, a sweet wheat preparation, appear in the documented repertoire. The arrangement should be distinguished from a prescribed dinner menu: an object can have a place on the display without constituting a course of the meal. [s3]\n\nThe history of the arrangement also requires caution. Encyclopaedia Iranica discusses variation in its contents and problems with projecting the familiar modern form unchanged into remote antiquity. Symbolic explanations should therefore be attributed to particular accounts rather than presented as one universally agreed code. [s3]\n\nFor the festive household, nuts, fruit, and sweets also serve the practical purpose of receiving visitors. Historical descriptions record both home preparation and purchases from confectioners, including small cookies made with rice or chickpea flour. This brings ceremonial food into the work of shopping, cooking, and entertaining as well as the display itself. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "festive-dishes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Special dishes and historical evidence",
        "content": "A study of Qajar-period cuisine documents herb rice, fish, and *kuku-ye sabzi*, an egg-and-herb dish, for Nowruz. It also records *shirin polow*, a festive rice preparation with sweet and aromatic additions, at wedding feasts. These are valuable historical associations, but the evidence does not establish a compulsory menu for contemporary Iranian families. [s1]\n\nThe historical context is important: a recipe manual or an account of an affluent household reveals a particular social world. It should not silently become a description of every region or income group. The article itself notes that meals differed according to occasion and household status. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "shared-foods",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Shared foods, different meanings",
        "content": "Ajil illustrates how flexible ceremonial eating can be. Salted or seasoned roasted mixtures differ from sweet mixtures combining raw nuts with dried fruit and other additions. The term therefore describes a family of preparations rather than a single standardized recipe. [s2]\n\nFor international readers, the most useful approach is to connect food with its setting instead of looking for an official national ritual menu. A New Year display, a wedding feast, and refreshments offered to visitors are related forms of hospitality, yet they organize food differently. Their overlap is part of the subject: the same ingredient may be ordinary refreshment in one gathering and carry ceremonial significance in another. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
