    "description": "Iranian clothing and everyday crafts connect material knowledge with household use, regional identities, and learned skills. Dress, woven textiles, carpets, baskets, pottery, and wooden objects belong to overlapping histories of making rather than a single uniform national style.",
    "tags": ["Regional dress", "Textiles", "Carpet weaving", "Basketry", "Pottery", "Woodworking", "Craft knowledge"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:26:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:26:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Clothing and Everyday Crafts in Iran: Materials, Regions and Skills",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran's regional dress, textiles, carpets, baskets, pottery, and wooden crafts, with attention to makers and evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "material-culture",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Useful objects and skilled work",
        "content": "Clothing and everyday crafts offer a way into Iranian culture through things people wear, store, carry, cook with, and place in their homes. Encyclopaedia Iranica defines crafts around handwork producing useful objects with artistic qualities. Its survey connects textiles, ceramics, and woodwork with changing markets as well as inherited techniques. These are histories of adaptation, not simply a catalogue of objects surviving unchanged. [s1]\n\nThe branches of this guide approach the subject through regional dress, local textile weaving, kilim and jajim, carpet weaving, baskets, pottery, and wooden handicrafts. Those headings overlap in practice: a textile can be clothing, household equipment, or part of a ceremonial setting. Following its material, construction, and use often explains more than placing it in a single decorative category. [s1][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-dress",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Regional dress requires context",
        "content": "There is no reliable shortcut from one garment to a complete identity. The scholarly study of Iranian clothing distinguishes construction, bodily position, occupation, social setting, and ceremonial use. Regional or ethnic labels can be informative, but they do not replace the details of who wore an item, when, and under what circumstances. [s2]\n\nEvidence also changes across periods. Actual garments survive unevenly; for earlier history, scholars often depend on texts and images. Courtly pictures can follow artistic conventions rather than reproduce everyday clothing faithfully. Collections may preserve exceptional garments more readily than ordinary working clothes. A museum piece, manuscript illustration, and field observation therefore answer different questions. [s2]\n\nThese distinctions are especially useful when encountering a photograph captioned simply “traditional Iranian dress.” A fuller account should identify the community and locality when verified, and distinguish daily clothing from festival or wedding attire. The aim is to recover a specific social setting rather than turn regional clothing into a timeless national uniform. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "weaving-and-needlework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Making cloth and working its surface",
        "content": "Textile production includes several different skills. Sericulture means raising silkworms for silk; UNESCO's description connects mulberry cultivation and silkworm care with reeling, preparing, and dyeing threads for weaving. Iran participates in the multinational heritage inscription for this practice. The shared listing is a reminder that textile knowledge crosses present-day borders. [s3]\n\nNeedlework is another distinct activity. UNESCO describes Turkmen-style needlework in Iran and Turkmenistan as decoration used on ordinary clothing, accessories, and garments for special occasions. Its account identifies regional variations in stitches and patterns, and learning through mothers and grandmothers. This provides a concrete example of dress as both an object and a body of knowledge transmitted through practice. [s4]\n\nHousehold textiles have equally varied uses. The Iranian cultural organization's account of jajim describes striped textiles used as covers, mats, and wrappers for bedding or belongings. Its regional examples show why a name alone does not establish one fixed appearance or function. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "carpet-making",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Carpets as a sequence of skills",
        "content": "Carpet weaving brings together work that is easy to overlook when attention rests only on the finished pattern. UNESCO's account of Fars describes wool preparation, loom construction, spinning, dyeing, weaving, and finishing. It documents a division of tasks within the communities concerned and learning through demonstration. That account should not be generalized to every Iranian workshop. [s5]\n\nThe same description emphasizes designs made without a drawn pattern guide. This distinguishes the documented practice from an assumption that all handmade carpets reproduce a fixed template. It also makes clear why safeguarding a craft concerns practical knowledge and opportunities to learn, as well as the preservation of completed objects. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "carpet-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A simplified Fars carpet-making sequence",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Shear wool\"] -->|\"Provides fibre\"| B[\"Spin yarn\"]\n  B -->|\"Prepare colours\"| C[\"Dye yarn\"]\n  D[\"Construct loom\"] -->|\"Provides working frame\"| E[\"Weave carpet\"]\n  C -->|\"Provides coloured yarn\"| E\n  E -->|\"Complete the textile\"| F[\"Finish and clean\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows distinguish material preparation from loom preparation before both contribute to weaving. This summarizes UNESCO's account of Fars and does not prescribe a universal Iranian production method.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "other-materials",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Beyond textiles",
        "content": "Basketry, pottery, and woodworking broaden the picture beyond carpets. The twentieth-century crafts survey records baskets made from reeds and other fibres, clay vessels produced through different forming and firing methods, and wooden furnishings and smaller implements. Materials and intended use help explain the differences between a storage container, cooking vessel, spoon, or decorated box. [s1]\n\nThese documented examples also need dates. A survey of twentieth-century craft production cannot establish the number of active makers or the commercial condition of a craft today. Likewise, recognition on a heritage list is evidence of recognition; it does not by itself measure household income, working conditions, or successful transmission. Reading objects alongside the limits of their documentation gives a more useful introduction to the people and skills behind them. [s1][s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
